    "finish": {
      "id": "finish",
      "type": "COMPLETION",
      "content": {
        "text": "Great work.\\n\\nYou now know the book's procedure for H.C.F. and L.C.M. of decimal fractions:\\n\\n1. Make all given numbers have the same number of decimal places by annexing zeros where necessary.\\n\\n2. Remove the decimal points.\\n\\n3. Find the H.C.F. or L.C.M. of the resulting whole numbers.\\n\\n4. Restore the decimal places in the result.\\n\\nBook examples to remember:\\n\\n0.63, 1.05, 2.1 → H.C.F. = 0.21 and L.C.M. = 6.30.\\n\\n1.75, 5.6, 7 → H.C.F. = 0.35.\\n\\n1.08, 0.36, 0.9 → H.C.F. = 0.18.\\n\\n0.54, 1.8, 7.2 → H.C.F. = 0.18.\\n\\n3, 2.7, 0.09 → L.C.M. = 27.\\n\\nThe biggest trap is stopping at the whole-number answer. Always restore the decimal places before giving the final answer.",
